{"id": "tea:vajasaneyi-samhita:1.1", "source": "src:vajasaneyi-samhita", "location": {"ref": "1.1", "chapter": "1", "verse": "1", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "The first formula of the White Yajurveda: (you twigs) for food, for strength; you are winds; may god Savitṛ impel you to the best of works; swell, inviolable cows, with Indra's share, rich in offspring, free from sickness and consumption; let no thief rule over you.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "ritual", "naya": null, "path": ["ritual"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["practice"], "terms": ["trm:yajus", "trm:savitr"], "practices": ["prc:darsapurnamasa"], "teachers": ["tch:yajnavalkya"], "reported_by_opponent": false, "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked", "checked_by": null, "date": null, "note": ""}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U01-vedic-samhitas"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U01-vedic-samhitas"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:vajasaneyi-samhita:1.5", "source": "src:vajasaneyi-samhita", "location": {"ref": "1.5", "chapter": "1", "verse": "5", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Agni, lord of vows, I shall undertake the vow; may I be able to do it; may it succeed for me; here I go from untruth to truth.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "seeker", "naya": null, "path": ["ritual", "action"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["ethics", "practice"], "original": {"text": "agne vratapate vrataṃ cariṣyāmi tac chakeyaṃ tan me rādhyatām | idam aham anṛtāt satyam upaimi", "script": "IAST", "edition": "Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā (Mādhyandina), standard text (IAST, accents omitted); wording checked against sources_raw/DharmicData", "licence": "public domain (ancient text)"}, "terms": ["trm:vrata", "trm:satya", "trm:anrta"], "concepts": ["cpt:satya-truth", "cpt:vrata-divine-ordinance"], "practices": ["prc:diksa-soma", "prc:darsapurnamasa"], "reported_by_opponent": false, "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked", "checked_by": null, "date": null, "note": ""}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U01-vedic-samhitas"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U01-vedic-samhitas"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:vajasaneyi-samhita:11.1-5", "source": "src:vajasaneyi-samhita", "location": {"ref": "11.1-5", "chapter": "11", "verse": "1-5", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "At the start of building the fire altar: Savitṛ, first yoking the mind (manas) and stretching out the thoughts (dhī) for truth (tattva), discerned the light of Agni and brought it from the earth; with yoked mind we are in the impulse of the god Savitṛ for strength to reach heaven; the inspired yoke their mind and their thoughts; 'I yoke with homage your ancient prayer'.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "seeker", "naya": null, "path": ["meditation", "ritual"], "stage": "intermediate"}, "types": ["practice", "consciousness-mind"], "terms": ["trm:manas", "trm:dhi", "trm:savitr"], "concepts": ["cpt:manas-vedic", "cpt:internalization-of-ritual"], "practices": ["prc:agnicayana"], "cross_refs": ["tea:rgveda:5.81.1", "tea:svetasvatara-upanisad:2.1-5"], "notes": "VS 11.1 checked ('yuñjānaḥ prathamaṃ manas tattvāya savitā dhiyaḥ'); = TS 4.1.1. The Śvetāśvatara Upaniṣad (2.1–5) quotes these verses at the head of its yoga chapter.", "reported_by_opponent": false, "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked", "checked_by": null, "date": null, "note": ""}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U01-vedic-samhitas"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U01-vedic-samhitas"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:vajasaneyi-samhita:16.25", "source": "src:vajasaneyi-samhita", "location": {"ref": "16.25", "chapter": "16", "verse": "25", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Homage to the hosts (gaṇa) and to the lords of hosts (gaṇapati), to the troops (vrāta) and their lords, to the skilled and their lords, to those of varied form and of every form.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "devotional", "naya": null, "path": ["sound"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["practice"], "terms": ["trm:rudra", "trm:namaka"], "concepts": ["cpt:rudra-vedic"], "practices": ["prc:rudra-japa"], "notes": "Checked in the local VS text.", "reported_by_opponent": false, "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked", "checked_by": null, "date": null, "note": ""}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U01-vedic-samhitas"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U01-vedic-samhitas"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:vajasaneyi-samhita:16.54", "source": "src:vajasaneyi-samhita", "location": {"ref": "16.54", "chapter": "16", "verse": "54", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "The countless thousands of Rudras upon the earth — their bows we unstring a thousand leagues away.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "devotional", "naya": null, "path": ["sound"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["practice"], "terms": ["trm:rudra"], "concepts": ["cpt:rudra-vedic"], "practices": ["prc:rudra-japa"], "notes": "Kautsa cites this verse against 'Rudra stood alone, there was no second' (TS 1.8.6) as an instance of mantras stating opposed things (vipratiṣiddha); Yāska answers (Nirukta 1.15–16).", "reported_by_opponent": false, "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked", "checked_by": null, "date": null, "note": ""}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U01-vedic-samhitas"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U01-vedic-samhitas"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:vajasaneyi-samhita:18.1", "source": "src:vajasaneyi-samhita", "location": {"ref": "18.1", "chapter": "18", "verse": "1", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "The opening of the Camaka in the White Yajurveda: 'food and impulse for me, effort and exertion, thought and will, sound and verse, hearing and learning …'.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "ritual", "naya": null, "path": ["ritual", "sound"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["practice"], "terms": ["trm:camaka"], "practices": ["prc:rudra-japa"], "cross_refs": ["tea:taittiriya-samhita:4.7.1-11"], "reported_by_opponent": false, "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked", "checked_by": null, "date": null, "note": ""}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U01-vedic-samhitas"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U01-vedic-samhitas"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:vajasaneyi-samhita:22.22", "source": "src:vajasaneyi-samhita", "location": {"ref": "22.22", "chapter": "22", "verse": "22", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "May a brāhmaṇa be born in the realm radiant with sacred knowledge; a rājanya, a hero, an archer, a great charioteer; a milch-cow, a draught-ox, a swift horse, a skilful woman, a victorious warrior, a youth fit for the assembly; may the rain fall when we wish; may our plants ripen; may we have security and prosperity.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "ethical-social", "naya": null, "path": ["general"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["ethics"], "terms": ["trm:brahman-priest"], "practices": ["prc:asvamedha"], "notes": "Checked (opening); prayer of the horse sacrifice.", "reported_by_opponent": false, "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked", "checked_by": null, "date": null, "note": ""}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U01-vedic-samhitas"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U01-vedic-samhitas"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:vajasaneyi-samhita:23.9-12", "source": "src:vajasaneyi-samhita", "location": {"ref": "23.9-12", "chapter": "23", "verse": "9-12", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Riddle-exchange (brahmodya) of the horse sacrifice: 'Who moves alone? Who is born again? What is the remedy for cold? What is the great field?' — 'The sun moves alone; the moon is born again; fire is the remedy for cold; the earth is the great field'; and further questions and answers.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "ritual", "naya": null, "path": ["ritual", "knowledge"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["practice", "sound-language"], "terms": ["trm:brahmodya"], "practices": ["prc:brahmodya", "prc:asvamedha"], "notes": "VS 23.9–10 checked; the range of the whole exchange from memory.", "reported_by_opponent": false, "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked", "checked_by": null, "date": null, "note": ""}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U01-vedic-samhitas"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U01-vedic-samhitas"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:vajasaneyi-samhita:26.2", "source": "src:vajasaneyi-samhita", "location": {"ref": "26.2", "chapter": "26", "verse": "2", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "As I speak this auspicious speech to the people — to brahmin and rājanya, to śūdra and ārya, to one's own and to the stranger — may I be dear to the gods and to the giver of gifts here.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "ethical-social", "naya": null, "path": ["sound"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["ethics", "teacher-transmission"], "terms": ["trm:vac"], "disputes": ["dsp:who-may-learn-the-veda"], "notes": "Checked. Dayānanda Sarasvatī cited this verse for the right of all to hear the Veda (recent reading).", "reported_by_opponent": false, "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked", "checked_by": null, "date": null, "note": ""}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U01-vedic-samhitas"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U01-vedic-samhitas"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:vajasaneyi-samhita:3.60", "source": "src:vajasaneyi-samhita", "location": {"ref": "3.60", "chapter": "3", "verse": "60", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Two forms of the Tryambaka verse: 'may I be freed from death, not from immortality', and, with 'finder of husbands' (pativedanam), 'may I be freed from here (the father's house), not from there'; followed (3.61) by sending Rudra with his provision beyond the Mūjavants, bow unstrung, clad in skin, auspicious (śiva).", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "ritual", "naya": null, "path": ["ritual", "sound"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["practice", "death-dying"], "terms": ["trm:tryambaka", "trm:rudra", "trm:siva"], "concepts": ["cpt:rudra-vedic", "cpt:mrtyu-death-and-its-bonds"], "practices": ["prc:mahamrtyunjaya-japa", "prc:caturmasya"], "notes": "Checked in the local VS text.", "reported_by_opponent": false, "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked", "checked_by": null, "date": null, "note": ""}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U01-vedic-samhitas"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U01-vedic-samhitas"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:vajasaneyi-samhita:3.62", "source": "src:vajasaneyi-samhita", "location": {"ref": "3.62", "chapter": "3", "verse": "62", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "The threefold life-span of Jamadagni, the threefold life-span of Kaśyapa, the threefold life-span that is among the gods — may that threefold life-span (tryāyuṣa) be ours.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "ritual", "naya": null, "path": ["ritual", "sound"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["practice", "death-dying"], "terms": ["trm:ayusya"], "concepts": ["cpt:full-life-hundred-autumns"], "notes": "Checked in the local VS text. Its later use in rites of tonsure and with the application of ash is recalled from memory (not recorded as teaching).", "reported_by_opponent": false, "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked", "checked_by": null, "date": null, "note": ""}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U01-vedic-samhitas"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U01-vedic-samhitas"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
